---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';

const generalCategories = [
  { value: 'Editorial or general', label: 'Editorial or general question about the book.' },
  { value: 'Press', label: 'Press or media inquiry.' },
  { value: 'Land opportunity', label: 'I have land that may fit the experiment criteria.' },
  { value: 'Capital or foundation', label: 'Capital, or a foundation relationship to bring.' },
  { value: 'Scientific collaboration', label: 'Scientific collaboration on the atlas or the experiment.' },
  { value: 'Correction or critique', label: 'A correction, an objection, or a sharper version of an argument.' },
  { value: 'Reader updates', label: 'I would like to be told when the book ships. No other mail.' },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Karen Pendergrass',
  jobTitle: 'Author; founder and director, Paleo Foundation',
  affiliation: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Paleo Foundation',
    url: 'https://paleofoundation.com',
  },
  email: 'mailto:karen@paleofoundation.com',
  url: 'https://sustainabilitydiet.com/about',
  description: 'Author of the forthcoming book The Sustainability Diet. Founder and director of the Paleo Foundation, which runs the Certified Paleo, Keto Certified, and Heavy Metal Tested & Certified (HMTc) programs.',
};
---

<Base
  title="About — The Sustainability Diet"
  description="About the site and the author. Karen Pendergrass, Paleo Foundation founder, and the long arc that became The Sustainability Diet."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-reading px-6 pt-20 pb-32">

    <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-4">About this site</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight leading-tight mb-12">
      What this site is, and why it exists.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose-reading text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/85 space-y-7">

      <p>
        This is the companion site to <em>The Sustainability Diet</em>,
        a forthcoming book by Karen Pendergrass. The book proposes a
        fourth axis for evaluating food systems &mdash;
        <em>survivability</em>, the question of how a system behaves
        when its inputs fail &mdash; and, following the axis honestly,
        arrives at a conclusion the food-systems conversation has
        mostly refused to reach: that sustainability at landscape
        scale requires the restoration of landscapes, and of the
        animals that once made them work.
      </p>

      <p>
        The site is not a supplement to the book. It is the part of
        the argument that did not belong on the page. The fossil
        occurrences, the orphan-fruit pairings, the rewilding projects
        already under way, and the experiment the book is asking
        someone to run are all more useful as interactive objects
        than as prose footnotes. Where the book makes the case in
        sentences, the site makes the case in maps, tables, and a
        proposal with a budget.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="dedication">
        In tribute to Paul S. Martin.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Paul Martin spent most of his career at the University of
        Arizona trying to put a single observation into wider
        circulation: that the ecological baseline conservation biology
        inherited from 1492 captures the moment of European contact
        and almost nothing ecological about the land itself. By the
        time Columbus made landfall, more than half the continent's
        large-bodied fauna had been absent for twelve and a half
        thousand years. The reference frame was already broken.
        Martin called the frame "the Columbian Curtain," and he
        proposed dropping it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The book exists because that argument, twenty years after
        <em>Twilight of the Mammoths</em>, is still outside most
        popular conservation conversation. The site exists because
        returning it to the conversation requires more than footnotes.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="author">
        About the author.
      </h2>

      <!-- Author block with headshot slot -->
      <div class="not-prose my-8 grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_2fr] gap-8 items-start">
        <div aria-hidden="true"
             class="border border-dashed border-ink/25 bg-bone/30 aspect-square flex items-center justify-center text-center p-6 text-ink/40">
          <div>
            <p class="small-caps text-xs mb-2">Author photo</p>
            <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed">
              Headshot placeholder.<br>
              Replace with /public/karen.jpg.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose-reading text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/85 space-y-5">
          <p>
            Karen Pendergrass is the founder and director of the
            <a href="https://paleofoundation.com" class="text-ochre hover:underline" rel="noopener">Paleo Foundation</a>,
            which she has run since 2012. Under the Foundation she
            built the Certified Paleo program, the Keto Certified
            program, and the Heavy Metal Tested &amp; Certified (HMTc)
            program for consumer packaged goods. She writes about
            food systems, heavy-metals toxicology, and the microbiome.
          </p>
          <p>
            She first drafted a version of this book fourteen years
            ago and is writing it for the first time now. The positions
            she held in 2012 are not the positions the current book
            defends; the intervening decade of microbiome research,
            heavy-metals work under the Foundation's certification
            program, and the slow accumulation of rewilding data have
            changed the argument substantially. Not being able to
            publish the first version turned out to be the thing that
            made this version possible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="paleo-foundation">
        On the relationship to the Paleo Foundation.
      </h2>

      <p>
        The book and the companion site are Karen's own work, written
        under her own name. The Paleo Foundation is not the publisher
        and the experiment on the <a href="/one-hundred-camels/the-experiment" class="text-ochre hover:underline">One Hundred Camels proposal page</a>
        is not a Foundation program. The Foundation's certification
        work gave this book its data on heavy-metals toxicology in
        the industrial food supply and on the microbiome consequences
        of current dietary patterns, and several of the book's
        factual claims are grounded in work done under the HMTc
        program. Everything beyond those factual inputs &mdash; the
        framework, the rewilding argument, the proposal &mdash; is
        personal.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="contact">
        Contact.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Write. The form below segments inbound by subject so each
        category reaches the right response path. Responses to
        experiment-related inquiries may take longer than press ones.
        Every note is read.
      </p>

      <div class="my-10">
        <ContactForm
          idPrefix="about"
          categories={generalCategories}
          subjectPrefix="[Sustainability Diet / "
        />
      </div>

      <p class="text-ink/70">
        If the form does not work for any reason, the direct address
        is <a href="mailto:karen@paleofoundation.com" class="text-ochre hover:underline">karen@paleofoundation.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="press">
        Press.
      </h2>

      <p class="text-ink/80">
        A press kit &mdash; author photo, book metadata, review copies,
        and a one-page press backgrounder &mdash; will be posted here
        ahead of publication. Reporters and editors who would like
        to be on the press list the day the kit posts should select
        <em>Press</em> on the form above.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight mt-14 mb-6" id="methodology">
        A note on the data.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Everything on the site sourced from a scientific database is
        attributed at the component level and in the methods block of
        the page it appears on. The site uses the Paleobiology Database
        (CC-BY), the Neotoma Paleoecology Database (CC-BY-4.0), USDA
        PLANTS (public domain), and GBIF and iNaturalist records
        filtered to permissive licenses. Where the science is actively
        contested &mdash; terminal-Pleistocene causation, the Younger
        Dryas impact hypothesis, specific plant&ndash;disperser
        pairings &mdash; the contest is named plainly and the
        strongest cases on both sides are cited. The full accounting
        is on the
        <a href="/near-time/sources" class="text-ochre hover:underline">sources &amp; methodology page</a>;
        the vocabulary is on the
        <a href="/near-time/glossary" class="text-ochre hover:underline">glossary page</a>.
      </p>

    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
